:Namespace MG

⎕IO←0 ⋄ ⎕CT←0

⍝ This problem is meant to get you used to thinking about recursive algorithms
⍝ in a more practical/real-world setting. This is a famous benchmark used 
⍝ in parallel computation called MG from the NPB benchmark suite. 
⍝ It is a method for computing a approximation of the discrete Poisson 
⍝ problem (∇2)u = v, specifically with periodic boundary conditions. 
⍝ 
⍝ You can read the details of the benchmark in the NPB reference document if 
⍝ you like. It specifies the following input parameters:

Sa←(¯3÷8) (÷32)(÷¯64) 0 
Sb←(¯3÷17)(÷33)(÷¯61) 0 

DS←256 ⋄ IT← 4 ⋄ S←Sa ⋄ k←2⍟DS ⍝ Class A
⍝ DS←256 ⋄ IT←20 ⋄ S←Sb ⋄ k←2⍟DS ⍝ Class B
⍝ DS←512 ⋄ IT←20 ⋄ S←Sb ⋄ k←2⍟DS ⍝ Class C

CI←+/|RI←⍉¯1+3 3 3⊤⍳27
A←(¯8÷3)  (0)  (÷6)(÷12)
P←(÷2     4    8     16)
Q←(÷1     2    4      8)

⍝ Computing the MG (multi-grid) consists of a series of operations that do 
⍝ a stencil computation. This stencil operation is specified in optimized 
⍝ form here, and expects to receive a specific kernel from the parameters above:

⍝ st←{⊃⍵{k x y z←⍺ ⋄ ⍵+k×x⊖y⌽[1]z⌽⍺⍺}⌿(⍺,¨RI),⊂(⍴⍵)⍴0}
st←{k←⍺
	x←⍵×k[0]
	x+←k[1]×(1⊖⍵)+(¯1⊖⍵)+r0←(1⌽⍵)+(¯1⌽⍵)+r01←(1⌽[1]⍵)+¯1⌽[1]⍵
	x+←k[2]×(1⊖r0)+(¯1⊖r0)+r1←(1⌽r01)+¯1⌽r01
	x+←k[3]×(1⊖r1)+¯1⊖r1
	x
}

⍝ For each recursion, we need to remember to downsample our input and upsample
⍝ the output as we progress through the recursive sub-calls. We have the 
⍝ following functions to do that for us.

dn←{⍵[i;i;i←1+2×⍳(≢⍵)÷2]}
up←{X⊣X[i;i;i←1+2×⍳≢⍵]←⍵⊣X←0⍴⍨2×⍴⍵}

⍝ Your job is to implement the recursive calls as well as the rest of the 
⍝ iteration. We have a combination of iteration and recursion here. 
⍝ The MG caller looks like this:

L2←{((+⌿,⍵×⍵)÷×⌿⍴⍵)*.5}
MG←{
	r←⍺ ∘∘∘ ⍵ ⍝ You implement this!
	L2 ⍺-A st r
}

⍝ You'll need to implement the iteration and the recursion. You'll need to call
⍝ the recursive M function IT times and evaluate the result r as follows:
⍝
⍝ r ←→ v - A st u
⍝ u ←→ u + k M r
⍝ 
⍝ Here's the definition of M according to NPB:
⍝
⍝ z_k = k M r_k :
⍝ 	if k > 1
⍝		r_k1 = P st r_k
⍝		z_k1 = (k-1) M r_k1
⍝		z_k  = Q st z_k1
⍝		r_k  = r_k - A st z_k
⍝		z_k  = z_k + S st r_k
⍝	else
⍝		z_1  = S st r_1
⍝
⍝ You'll need to turn that recursion into APL!

M←{∘∘∘}

⍝ The code below runs your solver. A fun little easter egg is the use of 
⍝ 128-bit floating point values to compute the random numbers for the inputs
⍝ to this solver using an RLCG random number generator. Fun stuff! 
⍝ 
⍝ To run the code, make sure the dataset size you've chosen above is the one 
⍝ you want, then call MG.Run⍬. 
	
seed←314159265
rlcg←{⎕FR←1287 ⋄ a←5*13 ⋄ b←⍺ ⋄ m←2*46 ⋄ z←(2*¯46)×{b∘←m|a×b}¨⍳⍵ ⋄ ⎕FR←645 ⋄ 1×z}
Gflops←{(58×IT×DS*3)÷2*30}

Run←{
	⎕←'Initializing data...'
	v←{(10⌿¯1 1)@(⊂[0](3⍴256)⊤(10↑r),¯10↑r←⍋seed rlcg DS*3)⊢(3⍴DS)⍴0}⍬
	u←DS DS DS⍴0
	
	⎕←'Running MG...'
	start←24 60 60 1000⊥¯4↑⎕TS
	r←v MG u
	end←24 60 60 1000⊥¯4↑⎕TS
	
	⎕←'MG time elapsed: ',(⍕time←(end-start)÷1000),'secs'
	⎕←'MG Gflops/s: ',⍕(Gflops⍬)÷time
	⎕←'L2 Norm: ',⍕r
	1:shy←Gflops time
}

:EndNamespace
